 "productionNotes": "No source consulted gives a reliable production total for the third-generation C/K, and the figures that circulate cannot all be true. Drivingline says more than a million examples were sold in the first year of production alone. A classic.com listing page says 1978 was the high-water mark with 1,317,466 trucks sold, and Classic Decoder says square body trucks passed 1.3 million units by 1978. Against that, the same Classic Decoder article says that by 1975 Chevrolet had sold over 75,000 square body trucks and calls it the best-selling product that year, a number that sits two orders of magnitude below the other sources and is not reconciled with them. High Flow Fuel estimates that over 10 million units were manufactured across the whole run. These figures use different scopes: some appear to cover Chevrolet and GMC together, some all weight classes and body styles including Blazer and Suburban, some only half-ton pickups, and none states its scope. No source consulted breaks out C10 half-ton pickups alone, regular cab against crew cab, Fleetside against Stepside, or two-wheel drive against four-wheel drive, so this page prints no total and no variant counts. The generation also straddles three naming eras: C/K through 1986, R/V for 1987, and R/V carryover trucks alongside the next-generation GMT400 truck through 1991, which this page covers only as a note. Wikipedia places production at 1972 through 1991 across several North American plants plus Argentina, Brazil and Chile, which is a wider window than the 1973 through 1987 model years classic.com and SlashGear use. Nothing here was obtained from the Chevrolet or GM archive, and no registry or club count was retrievable, so every number above is secondary.",
